	11.2 Student Self-Check Before Submission
* ☐ Title page, team details and revision history are complete
* ☐ Every requirement has a unique ID, priority and acceptance criteria
* ☐ No vague words (fast, easy, good, etc.) without measurable limits
* ☐ All figures are numbered, captioned and explained
* ☐ Use cases, class, sequence and activity diagrams are mutually consistent
* ☐ Traceability matrix covers all functional requirements
* ☐ Glossary covers all technical terms
* ☐ References follow one consistent style
* ☐ Document is proofread and plagiarism-free
* ☐ NPTEL/Coursera/Udemy etc.
11.3 NPTEL/Coursera/Udemy
* Paste certificate or Screen Shot of the course attended and completed.
* Course title and date must be clearly mention.
11.4 Common Mistakes to Avoid
1. Mixing design or implementation details into requirements (say what, not how).
2. Writing compound requirements ("and/or") that cannot be tested individually.
3. Drawing diagrams that do not match the written use cases.
4. Leaving out non-functional requirements.
5. Using inconsistent names for the same entity across sections.